! ALL THREE nonlinear lumped elements in one transient circuit: a PWM SWITCH, a
! DIODE and a ZENER, wired as a zener-clamped buck (demagnetization) cell.
!
! WHY THIS TEST EXISTS
! --------------------
! The three elements are stamped by three different mechanisms -- the switch
! from the clock, the diode from the coupled Newton iterate through the
! single-knee law, the zener through the two-knee law -- and each of the
! sibling tests exercises them one or two at a time in a circuit that has at
! most one nonlinear branch. This is the first test where all three share one
! circuit, where a single BRANCH holds two solution-dependent elements in
! series (so they must agree on one current while their voltages are solved
! separately), and where the circuit has FOUR nodes and TEN circuit variables.
!
! CIRCUIT (topology and sign conventions are drawn out in the companion
! nonlinear_combo_circuit.definitions)
!
!            +--- switch(2) ---+-------------+
!            |    S         A  |             |
!   source --+                 |           coil(1)
!            |                 |             |
!            |         zener(4)|             |
!            +------- M -------+             |
!            |  diode(3)                     |
!            +-------------------------------+
!                     G (return rail)
!
! EVERY ELEMENT HAS A DISTINCT, LOAD-BEARING JOB, and the companion reference
! script computes what happens when each is taken away:
!  * SWITCH: makes the ON/OFF phases at all. Closed, the source drives the coil
!    and node A sits at ~+9.2 V; open, the coil current has nowhere to go
!    through the switch (Roff) and pulls node A far below the return rail.
!  * DIODE: blocks the clamp branch during the ON phase. Without it the zener
!    sits straight across the coil and its Vf = 0 forward knee SHORTS the ON
!    phase -- the reference measures 52.0 A of shoot-through through the switch
!    and a coil that reaches only 4.05 A instead of 8.08 A.
!  * ZENER: opens the clamp branch during the OFF phase, in REVERSE BREAKDOWN
!    (not forward conduction -- it points the opposite way to the diode, see
!    the definitions file). Delete its "Diode Breakdown Voltage" and the two
!    elements become plain diodes in ANTI-SERIES: the branch cannot conduct in
!    either direction, node A is thrown to -61.4 V at the opening edge and the
!    coil current is gone within four steps.
!
! WHAT THE CLAMP DOES, AND WHY IT IS NOT JUST A FREEWHEEL. During the OFF phase
! the coil current commutates into the clamp branch, which holds node A at
!   -(Vz + Vf + I*(Ron_d + Ron_z)) = -6.31 V (ideal) / -6.88 V (solved)
! instead of the single forward drop -1.37 V a plain freewheel diode would
! give. The coil therefore demagnetizes MUCH faster: the reference measures
! 62.4% of the coil current removed in the 1 s OFF window against 39.1% for
! the single-diode freewheel of the sibling
! circuits2D_transient_switch_diode_freewheel, on the same mesh, same coil,
! same switch schedule and the same 0.7 V diode.
!
! TIMING. dt = Tsw/50 is the coarsest grid the switch documentation recommends
! and is used here on purpose (as in the freewheel sibling): the coupled
! iteration count at a switching edge scales with L*I/dt, so a finer grid would
! only make the test slower. phase = 3.6 deg = 0.01 of a period puts BOTH edges
! (opening at s = 0.81, closing at s = 1.01) exactly halfway between grid
! points, so the closed/open decision has half a timestep of margin and cannot
! be flipped by round-off in the accumulated time. Schedule: steps
! n mod 50 = 1..40 closed, 41..50 open. Three switching periods are run.
!
! DUTY = 0.8, NOT THE 0.4 OF THE FREEWHEEL SIBLING. A 5.7 V clamp against a
! 10 V source demagnetizes the coil about five times faster than a 0.7 V
! freewheel does, so at duty 0.4 (a 3 s OFF phase against L/R = 2.2 s) the coil
! current would reach ZERO 15 steps into every OFF phase and the cell would sit
! in discontinuous conduction: the "current at the end of an OFF phase"
! assertion would then be pinned on a number that is zero to within the diode
! leakage, and would assert nothing about the clamp. At duty 0.8 the cell stays
! in CONTINUOUS conduction -- 8.08 A at the end of the ON phase, 3.04 A at the
! end of the OFF phase -- so both asserted instants are far from zero and the
! whole excursion is governed by the clamp.
!
! WHAT IS ASSERTED, AND WHY FOUR INSTANTS-AND-QUANTITIES
! The run is split into two timestep intervals of 140 + 10 steps of the SAME
! step size, and the four SaveScalars solvers are gated with "Exec Intervals",
! exactly as in the freewheel sibling. Solvers 5 and 8 run only in the first
! interval, so the norms they leave behind are from step 140 = the END OF AN ON
! PHASE; solvers 6 and 7 run only in the second, so theirs are from step 150 =
! the END OF AN OFF PHASE. Same run, same solve, four numbers:
!   Solver 5  i_component(1) at step 140  -- the ON-phase coil current
!   Solver 8  i_component(3) at step 140  -- the blocking-phase clamp leakage
!   Solver 6  i_component(1) at step 150  -- the OFF-phase coil current, i.e.
!             the accelerated decay through the clamp
!   Solver 7  v_component(1) at step 150  -- the CLAMP VOLTAGE. The coil and
!             the clamp branch are the two branches between nodes A and G, so
!             the coil's own branch voltage IS the clamp branch voltage.
!
! ELEMENT MODELS (all three are the shipped laws, no new parameter regime)
!   switch  R(t) = Ron if MODULO(t*fsw - phase/360, 1) < duty else Roff
!   diode   R(V) = Ron + 0.5*(Roff-Ron)*(1 - TANH((V-Vf)/Vs))
!   zener   R(V) = Ron + 0.25*(Roff-Ron)*(1-TANH((V-Vf)/Vs))*(1+TANH((V+Vz)/Vs))
! Vs = 0.1 and Roff = 1e5 throughout, as in every sibling.
!
! SOLVER RECIPE: the Newton-era one. "Diode Newton Linearization" is left at
! its default True and "Nonlinear Circuit Residual Tolerance" gates the coupled
! exit on the components' own equations rather than on the field norm.
!
! THE RELAXATION FACTOR IS 0.3 HERE, NOT THE 0.7 OF THE ZENER SIBLING. Two
! solution-dependent elements in ONE branch are harder than one: they must
! agree on a shared current while each is linearized about its own relaxed
! iterate, and at a commutation the diode has to travel from -9.0 V to +1.33 V
! while the zener travels from +0.19 V to -5.55 V, both crossing saturated TANH
! regions in the same iteration. MEASURED over the full 150-step run with
! "Steady State Min Iterations = 3", the residual gate at 1e-8 and
! "Steady State Max Iterations = 100" (timesteps exhausting that limit / min /
! median / max coupled iterations / wall clock, OMP_NUM_THREADS = 1):
!   w = 1.0  ->  3/150   4/ 4/100    6.1 s   opening edges 41/91/141 chatter
!   w = 0.5  ->  9/150   4/ 6/100   11.7 s
!   w = 0.4  ->  3/150   4/ 7/100    8.9 s
!   w = 0.35 ->  2/150   5/ 7/100   10.4 s
!   w = 0.3  ->  0/150   5/ 9/ 70   10.4 s   <== used here
!   w = 0.25 ->  2/150   6/10/100   13.9 s   closing edges 51/101 run out of
!   w = 0.2  ->  5/150   7/12/100   16.1 s   the 100-iteration budget
!   w = 0.15 ->  5/150   8/16/100   19.7 s
!   w = 0.1  ->  8/150  12/24/100   28.9 s
!   w = 0.05 -> 44/150  21/46/100   52.3 s
! There are TWO failure modes and w = 0.3 is the only value of the sweep that
! clears both: above it the OPENING edges chatter in a limit cycle (too little
! damping for the commutation), below it nothing chatters but the crawl is so
! slow that the CLOSING edges exhaust the iteration budget instead. w = 0.3
! therefore sits between two failure modes rather than on a plateau, and its
! worst timestep (the closing edge, step 51) needs 70 of the 100 iterations --
! a 30% margin. If a future change ever pushes a step over the limit, the
! non-convergence WILL be reported: the residual gate turns a wrong operating
! mode into a loud failure, as the w = 1.0 row above shows.
! WHAT A FAILED OPENING EDGE COSTS, since the sweep makes it measurable: at
! w = 1.0 and w = 0.4 the loop leaves the edge in the WRONG operating mode --
! clamp branch still blocked -- and the coil current then sits on the 1.0e-04 A
! leakage floor for the rest of the OFF phase instead of at 3.04 A. The
! residual gate is what turns that into a reported non-convergence rather than
! a quiet wrong answer.
! LINEAR SYSTEM: a DIRECT solve. STEADY STATE CONVERGENCE IS RELATIVE, as in
! the freewheel sibling: a clamped buck cell in continuous conduction has no
! zero crossing (the coil current stays between 2.7 and 8.1 A after the first
! period), so a relative tolerance is well defined at every step. The zener
! sibling needs an ABSOLUTE one only because its sinusoidal source drives the
! field norm through zero twice a period.
!
! REFERENCE VALUES
! From the companion combo_reference.py in this directory, which shares no code
! with Elmer. Per step it reduces the whole cell to ONE scalar equation in the
! diode's own branch voltage, the clamp branch's shared current being obtained
! by inverting each element's characteristic i = V/R(V) (which is strictly
! monotone for both laws, so the inverse exists and is unique), and solves it
! by scanning for sign changes and bisecting -- never by Picard. The scan
! confirms EXACTLY ONE fixed point, and g strictly monotone, at every one of
! the 150 steps: three nonlinear elements and two knees have not introduced a
! second operating mode.
!   step 140, t = 14.00 s, END OF AN ON PHASE (switch closed):
!     i_coil = 8.0812231477 A, i_switch = 8.081313 A,
!     i_clamp = -9.0004208071e-05 A, v_coil = +9.1918686848 V,
!     v_diode = -9.000421 V (R = 1e5, blocking),
!     v_zener = +0.191448 V (R = 2.127e3, on its forward knee)
!   step 150, t = 15.00 s, END OF AN OFF PHASE (switch open):
!     i_coil = 3.0415318831 A, i_switch = 1.687839e-04 A,
!     i_clamp = +3.0413630992 A, v_coil = -6.8783891882 V,
!     v_diode = +1.329985 V (R = 0.4373, conducting),
!     v_zener = -5.548404 V (R = 1.824, in breakdown)
! Period-to-period drift of the two coil currents (the reference prints it):
!   end of ON  phase: 7.5816280142 -> 8.0320915159 -> 8.0812231477 A
!   end of OFF phase: 2.7133057680 -> 3.0092434842 -> 3.0415318831 A
! i.e. 0.6% and 1.1% between the second and third period -- the cell is close
! to, but not in, its periodic steady state, which is why the assertions are
! against a step-by-step reference and not against a steady-state formula.
!
! LIMITATIONS THIS TEST RECORDS RATHER THAN HIDES
!  * THE CLAMP KNEE IS SOFT, and softer than a single zener's. The solved
!    -6.8784 V sits 0.570 V = 5.7*Vs below the ideal
!    -(Vz + Vf + I*(Ron_d+Ron_z)) = -6.3083 V, because BOTH elements are on a
!    smoothed knee at once: the zener contributes 0.244 V and the series diode
!    another 0.326 V (its R is 0.4373 ohm, not Ron = 0.1). The clamp-voltage
!    assertion therefore carries a 2e-2 relative tolerance, twenty times looser
!    than the current assertions.
!  * THE BLOCKING IS CARRIED BY ONE Roff, NOT TWO. The naive estimate for two
!    blocking elements in series, |i_clamp| = |v_A|/(2*Roff), is wrong by a
!    factor 1.96 here: the zener's forward knee is at Vf = 0, so during the ON
!    phase it is pushed slightly FORWARD (+0.191 V) and contributes only
!    2.1e3 ohm against the diode's 1e5. The measured leakage is
!    0.979*|v_A|/Roff. That is a property of the settled parameter set, not a
!    defect -- it is recorded here because it is the kind of thing a reader
!    would otherwise assume the other way round.
!  * Ron is the same in both directions for both elements, and the same Vs
!    smooths every knee (see the Fortran).
!  * "r_component(N)" in the .dat files is the STAMPED resistance, i.e. R at
!    the relaxed iterate, so it lags the converged V slightly. The asserted
!    quantities are the solved dofs, not the stamped R.
!
! MEASURED COST over the full 150-step run: 0 of 150 timesteps exhaust "Steady
! State Max Iterations = 100" and the solver emits no non-convergence message;
! min 5 / median 9 / 90th percentile 20 / max 70 / mean 11.6 coupled iterations,
! 9.9 s wall clock under ctest. Only the five SWITCHING EDGES cost more than 30
! iterations -- opening 41/91/141 at 48/37/37 and closing 51/101 at 70/68 --
! and every other step is in single or low double digits.
!
! Based on circuits2D_transient_switch_diode_freewheel and
! circuits2D_transient_zener_clamp.
Check Keywords "Warn"
INCLUDE sif/nonlinear_combo_circuit.definitions
INCLUDE 1381/mesh.names
Header 1
   Mesh DB "." "1381"
End

$ V0 = 10        ! DC source [V]
$ fsw = 0.2      ! switching frequency [Hz]
$ Tsw = 1/fsw
$ nper = 50      ! timesteps per switching period; dt = Tsw/50 is the coarsest
                 ! grid the switch documentation recommends -- see TIMING
$ dt = Tsw/nper
! Interval 1 ends at the last CLOSED step of period 3, interval 2 at the last
! OPEN step of period 3. They must be given to Elmer as a single MATC VECTOR:
! an inline "$" substitution consumes the rest of the line, so
! "Timestep Intervals(2) = $ non $ noff" leaves the second name unsubstituted
! and the sif parser rejects it.
$ nn = zeros(1,2)
$ nn(0,0) = 140
$ nn(0,1) = 10
$ duty = 0.8     ! duty cycle -- see DUTY = 0.8 above
$ phase = 3.6    ! [deg] = 0.01 period, puts both edges midway between steps
$ Rons = 0.1     ! switch on-state resistance [ohm]
$ Roffs = 1.0e5  ! switch off-state resistance [ohm]
$ Vfd = 0.7      ! series diode forward voltage [V]
$ Rond = 0.1     ! series diode on-state resistance [ohm]
$ Roffd = 1.0e5  ! series diode off-state resistance [ohm]
$ Vz = 5.0       ! zener breakdown voltage [V]
$ Vfz = 0.0      ! zener forward voltage [V]
$ Ronz = 0.1     ! zener on-state resistance [ohm]
$ Roffz = 1.0e5  ! zener blocking-window resistance [ohm]
$ Vs = 0.1       ! smoothing voltage, both elements, every knee [V]
$ wrel = 0.3     ! Circuit Variable Relaxation Factor, see SOLVER RECIPE
$ crtres = 1.0e-8 ! circuit-residual tolerance, see Solver 1
$ sstol = 1.0e-9

Simulation 1
   Max Output Level = 5
   Coordinate System = Axi symmetric
   Simulation Type = Transient
   Timestepping Method = BDF
   BDF Order = 1
!  Two intervals of the SAME step size; the split exists only so that the four
!  SaveScalars solvers below can be gated to two different instants.
   Timestep Sizes(2) = $ dt*ones(1,2)
   Timestep Intervals(2) = $ nn
!  > 1 enables the within-timestep coupled loop over the two solution-dependent
!  resistances. Measured: min 5, median 9, max 70 iterations per timestep, 0 of
!  150 exhaust this limit.
   Steady State Max Iterations = 100
!  A FLOOR, not the convergence criterion -- that is the circuit residual on
!  Solver 1.
   Steady State Min Iterations = 3
End
Solver 1
   Exec Solver = Always
   Equation = Circuits
   Procedure = "CircuitsAndDynamics" "CircuitsAndDynamics"
!  NOTE: "Export Circuit Variables" is deliberately NOT set. None of the three
!  elements needs it -- the diode and the zener read their own branch voltages
!  from the solver's internal iterate and the switch reads the clock.
   Circuit Variable Relaxation Factor = Real $ wrel
!  The coupled loop cannot exit while EITHER solution-dependent component still
!  violates its own equation R(V)*I - V = 0 by more than this (scaled) amount;
!  the published residual is the MAX over components, so the diode and the
!  zener are gated together. "Exec Solver = Always" above is required for this
!  to have any effect at all.
   Nonlinear Circuit Residual Tolerance = Real $ crtres
End
Solver 2
   Equation = "Mag"
   Variable = A
   Procedure = "MagnetoDynamics2D" "MagnetoDynamics2D"
   NonLinear System Relaxation Factor=1
   Export Lagrange Multiplier = Logical True
   Linear System Solver = Direct
   Linear System Direct Method = UMFPACK
!  Relative, see LINEAR SYSTEM above: the coil current never passes through
!  zero in this cell.
   Steady State Convergence Tolerance = $ sstol
End
Solver 3 ! Result output
   Exec Solver = Never
   Equation = "ResultOutput"
   Procedure = "ResultOutputSolve" "ResultOutputSolver"
   Output File Name = 1381-results
   Vtu format = Logical True
   Save Geometry Ids = Logical True
End
Solver 4
   Exec Solver = Always
   Equation = Circuits Output
   Procedure = "CircuitsAndDynamics" "CircuitsOutput"
End
Solver 5
   Exec Solver = After Timestep
!  Runs during the FIRST timestep interval only, so the norm it leaves behind
!  is the one from its last execution: step 140, the end of an ON phase.
   Exec Intervals(2) = 1 0
   Equation = "sv on"
   Procedure = "SaveData" "SaveScalars"
   Filename = 1381/dat/combo_on.dat
   Show Norm Name = String "res: i_component(1)"
End
Solver 6
   Exec Solver = After Timestep
!  Runs during the SECOND timestep interval only: last execution is step 150,
!  the end of an OFF phase.
   Exec Intervals(2) = 0 1
   Equation = "sv off"
   Procedure = "SaveData" "SaveScalars"
   Filename = 1381/dat/combo_off.dat
   Show Norm Name = String "res: i_component(1)"
End
Solver 7
   Exec Solver = After Timestep
   Exec Intervals(2) = 0 1
   Equation = "sv clamp"
   Procedure = "SaveData" "SaveScalars"
   Filename = 1381/dat/combo_clamp.dat
!  THE CLAMP VOLTAGE. The coil and the clamp branch are the two branches
!  between nodes A and G, so the coil's own branch voltage is the voltage
!  across the clamp branch.
   Show Norm Name = String "res: v_component(1)"
End
Solver 8
   Exec Solver = After Timestep
   Exec Intervals(2) = 1 0
   Equation = "sv leak"
   Procedure = "SaveData" "SaveScalars"
   Filename = 1381/dat/combo_leak.dat
!  THE BLOCKING-PHASE LEAKAGE of the clamp branch, taken on the diode, which is
!  the element that does the blocking.
   Show Norm Name = String "res: i_component(3)"
End
Equation 1
   Active Solvers(2) = 2 3
End
Equation 2
   Active Solvers(3) = 1 2 3
End
Material 1
   Name = iron
   Electric Conductivity = 0
   Relative Permeability = 2000
End
Material 2
   Name = air
   Electric Conductivity = 0
   Relative Permeability = 1
End
Material 3
   Name = aluminium
   Relative Permeability = 1
   Electric Conductivity = 1e7
End
Body 1
   Name = Core
   Target Bodies(6) = 1 2 3 4 5 6
   Equation = 1
   Material = 1
End
Body 2
   Name = air
   Target Bodies(2) = 9 7
   Equation = 1
   Material = 2
End
Body 3
   Name = test 1
   Target Bodies = 8
   Equation = 2
   Material = 3
End
Component 1
   Name = String test inductor
   Master Bodies = Integer 3
   Coil Type = String stranded
   Number of Turns = Real 1000
!  Explicit coil resistance: suppresses the FEM sigma-based resistance so that
!  the clamp loop resistance is exactly 1 ohm + R_diode + R_zener.
   Resistance = Real 1.0
End
Component 2
   Name = String pwm switch
   Component Type = String Switch
   Switch Frequency = Real $ fsw
   Switch Duty Cycle = Real $ duty
   Switch Phase = Real $ phase
   Switch On Resistance = Real $ Rons
   Switch Off Resistance = Real $ Roffs
End
Component 3
   Name = String series diode
   Component Type = String Diode
!  The PLAIN single-knee law: no "Diode Breakdown Voltage". Its job is to block
!  the clamp branch during the ON phase, where it sees only -9.0 V, so a
!  breakdown knee would have to be added deliberately.
   Diode Forward Voltage = Real $ Vfd
   Diode On Resistance = Real $ Rond
   Diode Off Resistance = Real $ Roffd
   Diode Smoothing Voltage = Real $ Vs
End
Component 4
   Name = String clamp zener
   Component Type = String Diode
!  The TWO-knee law: "Diode Breakdown Voltage" is the only keyword that
!  separates a zener from the plain diode of component 3. This element points
!  the OPPOSITE way round in the branch (see the definitions file), so it is
!  the breakdown knee, not the forward knee, that carries the clamp current.
   Diode Forward Voltage = Real $ Vfz
   Diode Breakdown Voltage = Real $ Vz
   Diode On Resistance = Real $ Ronz
   Diode Off Resistance = Real $ Roffz
   Diode Smoothing Voltage = Real $ Vs
End
Body Force 1
   Name = "Circuit"
!  Constant DC drive: all the structure in the waveform comes from the switch.
   testsource = Real $ V0
End
Boundary Condition 1
   Target Boundaries(4) = $ center0 center1 center2 center3
   A = Real 0
End
Boundary Condition 2
   Target Boundaries(2) = 4 6
   Name = "Air Gap"
   Air Gap = logical True
   Air Gap Length = Real 0.0001
   Air Gap Relative Permeability = Real 1
End
Boundary Condition 3
   Target Boundaries(7) = 10 13 14 17 18 19 27
   Infinity BC = Logical True
End

! The Solver 2 norm at the final step. It is the nonlinear-system norm over the
! Lagrange-augmented solution vector (field dofs plus the ten circuit dofs).
Solver 2 :: Reference Norm = 7.60430168E-03
Solver 2 :: Reference Norm Tolerance = Real 1E-04
! Independent lumped BDF1 references, see REFERENCE VALUES above. SaveScalars
! reports the norm of the saved value, so negative quantities are asserted as
! MAGNITUDES -- exactly as in the sibling circuits2D_transient_zener_clamp.
! Solver 5: coil current at step 140, the end of an ON phase.
Solver 5 :: Reference Norm = 8.0812231477
Solver 5 :: Reference Norm Tolerance = Real 1E-03
! Solver 6: coil current at step 150, the end of an OFF phase. This is the one
! that pins the ACCELERATED DECAY: the single-diode freewheel of the sibling
! test would still be at 5.13 A here.
Solver 6 :: Reference Norm = 3.0415318831
Solver 6 :: Reference Norm Tolerance = Real 1E-03
! Solver 7: the CLAMP VOLTAGE at step 150, -6.8783891882 V against an ideal
! -(Vz + Vf + I*(Ron_d+Ron_z)) = -6.3083 V. The 0.570 V gap is the smoothed
! knees of BOTH clamp elements, hence the looser tolerance -- see LIMITATIONS.
Solver 7 :: Reference Norm = 6.8783891882
Solver 7 :: Reference Norm Tolerance = Real 2E-02
! Solver 8: the clamp-branch leakage at step 140, -9.0004208071e-05 A. This is
! a MAGNITUDE/CEILING check on a quantity whose whole point is that it stays in
! the |v_A|/Roff decade (= 9.19e-05 A here), so the tolerance is deliberately
! loose. Elmer's framework offers only a two-sided relative band, so a 10%
! band is what "ceiling" is expressed as.
Solver 8 :: Reference Norm = 9.0004208071E-05
Solver 8 :: Reference Norm Tolerance = Real 1E-01
